course: dictionary
lesson: 4939
title: Impose: rules, pushing ideas, and being a bother
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impose is a verb, and it has three main meanings. It can mean to make a rule, tax or punishment that people must accept, like a council that imposes a speed limit. It can mean to force your ideas or wishes on other people. It can also mean to ask too much of someone and cause them trouble.
explain: Use the context to choose the meaning. In «Do not impose on others what you do not wish for yourself», the words «on others» and «what you do not wish for yourself» tell you that impose here means forcing something on people that they would not choose. Try the swap test: put «force» or «make them accept» into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Impose on someone has a polite meaning too. «I don't want to impose» means «I don't want to be a bother». When you read impose, ask: is someone being made to accept a rule, is someone's idea being forced, or is someone being a bother? The sentence will tell you which one.
fact: Impose means to put a rule, tax or punishment on people, and they must accept it.
fact: Impose can also mean to force your ideas or wishes on other people.
fact: «I don't want to impose» means «I don't want to be a bother».
fact: Imposing, the adjective, means large and impressive, like an imposing castle.
fact: The forms are impose, imposes, imposed and imposing.
exercise: The council will impose a new speed limit on the road. What does impose mean here? | Paint the road a new colour | Feel very tired | *Make a rule that people must accept | Ask people to sing a song
exercise: «Do not impose on others what you do not wish for yourself.» What does impose mean in this quote? | *Force on others | Explain slowly | Give a present | Forget something
exercise: Sam says, «I don't want to impose.» What does Sam mean? | He wants to paint the house | *He doesn't want to be a bother | He is very hungry | He wants to be in charge of everyone
test: Your teacher imposes a detention for talking in class. Which meaning fits? | *Gives a punishment that must be accepted | Sings loudly | Makes a cake | Gives a prize
test: Which word is the adjective that means large and impressive? | *Imposing | Impose | Imposed | Imposes
exam: Which sentence uses impose to mean asking too much of someone? | I like apples and bananas | The bus is late again | The sun is shining brightly today | *Please don't impose on your neighbour by borrowing their car every day
